---
/*  What the encoder adds to the window every plugin shares: its path, a custom path's nodes, where the path
    sits, how the track travels it, and what it draws on the scene. The pictures are the encoder's own, drawn
    by its picture tool. */
import TutorialPage from '../../layouts/TutorialPage.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const img = (name: string) => `${base}/tutorial/${name}.webp`;
const window = `${base}/tutorial/the-window`;

const parts: [string, string][] = [
  ['trajectory', 'trajectory'],
  ['custom', 'a custom path'],
  ['transform', 'transform'],
  ['source', 'source'],
  ['scene', 'the scene'],
  ['try', 'things to try'],
];
---

<TutorialPage
  id="encoder"
  sub="a source that travels a path on the sphere, moved by what it plays"
  parts={parts}
  window={{ src: img('encoder-window'), alt: 'An encoder window: five tracks as tabs, the scene with the paths, the matrix with three cells set, and the trajectory tab.', caption: 'an encoder window' }}
>
  <div class="prose" slot="intro">
    <p class="lede">
      The encoder places a mono or stereo track in the ambisonic field and moves it along a path you draw on the
      sphere. How fast it travels can follow the input: silence parks the source, loud material moves it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Its window is the one every bambi plugin has; the scene, the matrix, regions, presets and the link are
      explained in <a href={window}>the window</a>. This page covers what is the encoder's own: its three tabs,
      <code>trajectory</code>, <code>transform</code> and <code>source</code>, and what it draws on the scene.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="trajectory" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('encoder-trajectory')} alt="The trajectory tab: lissajous chosen, its az and el amount, az and el ratio, phase, and convert to custom." width="696" height="700" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>trajectory</h2>
      <p>
        The path's shape. The row at the top chooses a kind, and under it are that kind's own settings:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><code>orbit</code></strong>: <code>tilt</code>, <code>axis az</code> and <code>aperture</code>.</li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>lissajous</code></strong>: <code>az amount</code> and <code>el amount</code>, how far it swings
          each way; <code>az ratio</code> and <code>el ratio</code>, how many times; and <code>phase</code>.
        </li>
        <li><strong><code>wave</code></strong>: <code>turns</code>, <code>el amount</code>, <code>wobbles</code> and <code>el offset</code>.</li>
        <li><strong><code>arc</code></strong>: <code>centre az</code>, <code>centre el</code>, <code>length</code> and <code>heading</code>.</li>
        <li><strong><code>spiral</code></strong>: <code>turns</code>, <code>from el</code>, <code>to el</code> and <code>start az</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The angles can be automated and modulated, so a path can breathe with the music. The whole numbers —
        ratios, turns, wobbles — jump rather than sweep, so they are kept with the instance and not automated.
        Choosing another kind resets its whole numbers to their defaults; its angles stay as you last set them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="custom" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('encoder-custom')} alt="The trajectory tab on a custom path: closed or open, node 3 of 8, smooth or corner, delete and make start." width="696" height="484" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>a custom path</h2>
      <p>
        <code>convert to custom</code> turns the path into nodes you edit on the sphere. The kind you converted
        from is kept: choose it again and its settings come back as they were. <code>custom</code> is offered once
        you have converted, and converting again replaces the custom path you have.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Drag a node</strong> to move it, and its handles to bend the curve. One drag is one undo step.</li>
        <li><strong>Alt-drag a handle</strong> to make a corner.</li>
        <li><strong>Click the curve</strong> to add a node there.</li>
        <li><strong>Shift-click a node</strong> to delete it. A path keeps at least two nodes, and holds up to 24.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The tab holds the same, in words:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><code>closed</code></strong> or <strong><code>open</code></strong>: whether the path joins up.</li>
        <li>For the chosen node: <strong><code>smooth</code></strong> or <strong><code>corner</code></strong>, and <strong><code>delete</code></strong>.</li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>make start</code></strong>, on a closed path, makes the chosen node the first: where the
          path is cut when it is opened.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
  <figure>
    <img src={img('encoder-nodes')} alt="The scene with a custom path: its nodes as white circles, the chosen one with its handles." width="1236" height="602" />
  </figure>

  <section id="transform" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('encoder-transform')} alt="The transform tab: yaw, pitch and roll, each with a rate, and extent." width="696" height="640" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>transform</h2>
      <p>Where the path sits and how it turns, whatever its kind:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong><code>yaw</code></strong>, <strong><code>pitch</code></strong> and <strong><code>roll</code></strong>,
          each beside a <strong>rate</strong> in degrees a second that keeps it turning.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>extent</code></strong>, from 0 to 2: at 1 the path is as drawn, at 0 it closes onto its own
          centre, and at 2 it opens out through the far side.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="source" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('values')} alt="The source tab: sum, mid/side and stereo; spread, offset and input trim; motion; render." width="696" height="1116" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>source</h2>
      <p>How the track sits on the path and travels it, in three groups.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The input.</strong> <code>sum</code> puts one point on the path. <code>mid/side</code> puts the
          mid on the path and the side either side of it, <code>spread</code> apart. <code>stereo</code> puts left
          and right on the path, <code>offset</code> apart. <code>input trim</code>, ±24 dB, sets the level before
          the features hear it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>motion</strong>. <code>speed</code> is how fast the source travels, in degrees a second, and
          <code>displace</code> is where on the path it sits. <code>direction</code> is forward or reverse, and
          <code>mode</code> says what happens at the ends: <code>wrap</code>, <code>ping-pong</code> or
          <code>once</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>render</strong>. <code>width</code>, from a point to 180°, is how far the source spreads over
          the sphere, and <code>width range</code> is the range modulation may move it in.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="scene">
    <h2>the scene</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src={img('encoder-scene')} alt="The scene: the selected track's path in ink with its source on it, the other tracks' paths beside it." width="1236" height="602" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The path is drawn in ink on the globe and the equirect, with the source on it in the colour of what comes
        in. Every other encoder in the session is drawn beside it, fainter, so you place each track against the
        others. <strong>Click another track's source or path</strong> to switch the window to that instance, as its tab in
        the header does.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="try">
    <h2>things to try</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>The kick nudges on every hit.</strong> Choose <code>arc</code>, click <code>displace</code>, click
          the cell under <code>attack</code>, and drag it up.
        </li>
        <li><strong>The pad opens up when it rings.</strong> Click <code>width</code>, click the cell under <code>tonal</code>, and drag.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>It drifts slowly, and faster when it is loud.</strong> Set <code>speed</code> to a small value,
          then click <code>speed</code> and give it depth under <code>level</code>.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
</TutorialPage>
